# A platform to make geospatial data more accessible

The **DSP — Data Sharing Platform** is a generic platform for public consultation of geospatial data. Inspired by the experience of the [Rural Environmental Registry Public Consultation (CAR)](https://consulta.car.gov.br/), it was designed so that different organizations can publish, explore, and share their own data through a reusable and configurable solution.

*Digital Public Good · Open Source · Reusable*

## The DSP in brief

- Public consultation of geospatial data.
- Configurable instances without code changes.
- Hierarchical territorial filters.
- Configurable map, layers, KPIs, and downloads.
- GeoServer integration.
- Collaborative evolution.

## What is the DSP?

The Data Sharing Platform is a generic product designed to turn the public consultation experience for geographic data into a reusable capability for different institutions.

The [CAR Public Consultation](https://consulta.car.gov.br/) shows how an interface can combine filters, indicators, cartographic visualization, layers, and data publishing. The DSP generalizes that capability so the solution is not limited to a single database or domain.

Instead of relying on territorial values, indicators, or layers fixed in code, a DSP instance can define its own structure by running `./config.sh`, which guides configuration and generates `config/adopter/adopter-config.yaml`.

For example, one instance can use **Country → Region → District**, while another can use **State → Municipality → Area**, without changing application logic.

## Why use the DSP?

### Reusable

The same technology base can serve different organizations, territories, and datasets.

### Configurable

Territorial structure, filters, KPIs, and layers are driven by configuration, including integration with the [RER](https://www.digitalpublicgoods.net/r/rural-environmental-registry-registration-module) as a Digital Public Good.

### Transparent and auditable

As a digital public good, its evolution can be followed and improved by the community.

## Where can the DSP be used?

### Public agencies

Active transparency and publication of territorial information.

### Environment

Consultation of areas, records, themes, and monitoring data.

### Territorial management

Exploration of data across different territorial levels.

### Research and analysis

Access to data and layers to support studies and new services.

## A product designed to be shared

The DSP was conceived as a Digital Public Good: a reusable, adaptable, and evolving solution for different contexts, so each institution does not need to start from scratch.

## Help build the DSP

A Digital Public Good grows stronger with those who use, implement, research, and develop it. Contribute by reporting issues, proposing features, sharing deployment experiences, and submitting improvements.

- [Access the GitHub repository](https://github.com/Rural-Environmental-Registry/dsp-core)
